er><h1>Pair Sum - Unsorted</h1></header><div class=3D"style-module-scss-mod=
ule__ubvNPq__problemDescription"><p>Given an array of integers, return the =
indexes of <strong>any two numbers that add up to a target</strong>. The or=
der of the indexes in the result doesn't matter. If no pair is found, retur=
n an empty array.</p><h4 id=3D"example">Example:</h4><pre><code class=3D"hl=
js language-python">Input: nums =3D [-<span class=3D"hljs-number">1</span>,=
 <span class=3D"hljs-number">3</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">4</span>=
, <span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>], target =3D <span class=3D"hljs-num=
ber">3</span>
Output: [<span class=3D"hljs-number">0</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">=
2</span>]
</code></pre><p>Explanation: <code>nums[0] + nums[2] =3D -1 + 4 =3D 3</code=
></p><h4 id=3D"constraints">Constraints:</h4><ul>
<li>The same index cannot be used twice in the result.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>A brute force approach is to iterate through every possible pair in the =
array to see if their sum is equal to the target. This is the same as the b=
rute force solution described in the <em>Pair Sum - Sorted</em> problem, wh=
ich has a time complexity of <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mat=
hml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><m=
i>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo s=
tretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">O=
(n^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria=
-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height=
:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" st=
yle=3D"margin-right:0.02778em">O</span><span class=3D"mopen">(</span><span =
class=3D"mord"><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span><span class=3D"msups=
ub"><span class=3D"vlist-t"><span class=3D"vlist-r"><span class=3D"vlist" s=
tyle=3D"height:0.8141em"><span style=3D"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em"><=
span class=3D"pstrut" style=3D"height:2.7em"></span><span class=3D"sizing r=
eset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class=3D"mord mtight">2</span></span></span>=
</span></span></span></span></span><span class=3D"mclose">)</span></span></=
