:0.03588em">g</span><span class=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord mathn=
ormal">n</span><span class=3D"mclose">))</span></span></span></span> time d=
ue to sorting. Let=E2=80=99s see if we can find an even faster solution.</p=
>
<p><strong>Complement</strong><br>
<!-- -->We're asked to find a pair (<code>x</code>, <code>y</code>) such th=
at <code>x + y =3D=3D target</code>. In this equation, there are two unknow=
ns: <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>. An important observation is that if =
we know one of these numbers, we can easily calculate what the other number=
 should be.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For each number x in nums, we need to find another number <code>y</code>=
 such that <code>x + y =3D target</code>, or in other words, <code>y =3D ta=
rget - x</code>. We can call this number the complement of <code>x</code>.<=
/p>
</blockquote>
<p>Keep in mind, we need to return the indexes of the pair of numbers, not =
the pair itself. So, we=E2=80=99ll need a way to find a number's complement=
 as well as its index.</p>
<p>One way we could do this is to loop through the array to find each numbe=
r=E2=80=99s complement and corresponding index. But this takes <span class=
